      "message": "OK, right! It\u0027s a bit confusing to me, though I think your comment describes the situation well. But to spell it out even more for myself I guess `1 - diffRadius^2` (or equivalently `1 - abs(diffRadius)`) is always negative when one circle is inside the other, ignoring order. `1 - diffRadius` ends up negative when circle 0 is inside circle 1 and positive when circle 1 is inside circle 0; it has the opposite sign of `diffRadius` in these cases.\n\nSince we want a sign of `t` such that we have a positive interpolated radius, `t`\u0027s sign should match the sign of `diffRadius`, which it does because we use `1 - diffRadius` and we multiply by `invA` which is negative. All that said, I\u0027m now convinced what we have here is good 😄",
